The sign vectors for the best-found $N=4,5$ cases are provided in a matrix representation in Fig.~\ref{fig:sign_matrix}. 
Together with $\gamma$ values shown in Table~\ref{tab:tab}, they allow an explicit reconstruction of the desired states up to $N\leq 5$ qubits. Explicit sign vectors and parametrised states are provided in Appendix~\ref{app} for convenience.

For $N=4$, the best $\gamma$ was found to be $\approx 0.067$ with $\eta_S \approx 1.14$, which signals non-stabiliserness in the state. In contrast to the Pauli flat state of two qubits, it cannot be generated by tracing out ancillary parties from a larger pure Pauli flat state. Its entanglement can again be inferred from the PPT criterion. The considered state is definitely entangled as, for each bipartition, the sum of all negative eigenvalues ranges between $0.066$ and $0.096$. Despite that, it is either not, or very weakly, genuinely multipartite entangled. This was verified by the monotone from~\cite{Jungnitsch_2011} using the PPTMixer tool~\cite{bastian2026pptmixer}.
A four-partite state Bell nonlocality is significantly harder to examine. However, limiting oneself to two and three settings per party, it becomes feasible. An especially interesting class of inequalities concerning Pauli flat states are Bell inequalities that can be violated with Pauli measurements only.  Using techniques from~\cite{Cieslinski_2026} it can be verified that, despite having all non-zero correlations, it does not lead to a violation of any two- and three-settings Bell inequality of this type.
At last, for $N=5$ an appropriate Pauli flat state was found, but the corresponding $\gamma$ was too small to yield any magic within it. Due to its correlation structure and no detectable magic, we regard its operational meaning as magic mimicking. Despite that and a purity of $\approx 0.06$ it is still entangled, and its negativity over all bipartitions yields values within $[0.05,0.066]$, which has a non-zero overlap with the values obtained for $N=4$. Using the same techniques as before, the considered state is concluded to have no genuine multipartite entanglement.

\subsection{Relaxation on the number of equal correlations}
\label{sec:pure_states_relax}

\begin{figure}
