\title{Energy-constrained two-way capacity bounds for noisy Gaussian channels}
\author{Stefano Pirandola}
\affiliation{Department of Computer Science, University of York, York YO10 5GH, United Kingdom}
\begin{abstract}
A gap between achievable rates and converse bounds persists for
energy-constrained bosonic communication in the presence of excess noise.
We derive a unified adaptive weak-converse bound for thermal attenuation,
noisy amplification, and additive Gaussian noise under an unconditional
mean transmitted-photon-number constraint. The bound applies to arbitrary
adaptive protocols with quantum memories and constrains the two-way
quantum, entanglement-distribution, private, and secret-key capacities.
It vanishes throughout the entanglement-breaking region and approaches
the corresponding PLOB bound at infinite energy. At finite energy, it
improves the evaluated Gaussian squashed-entanglement and PLOB bounds in
relevant parameter regimes, while comparison with hashing rates quantifies
the remaining gap to achievability. The result provides a common
energy-dependent benchmark for noisy bosonic Gaussian channels and extends
finite-energy converse methods beyond the quantum-limited setting.
\end{abstract}
\maketitle

\section{Introduction}\label{sec:introduction}
Thermal attenuation, noisy amplification, and additive Gaussian noise are
basic models of imperfect bosonic communication~\cite{HolevoWerner,Weedbrook}.
Their energy-constrained two-way-assisted capacities describe the
ultimate rates of quantum communication and secret-key generation with
unlimited authenticated public classical communication at a prescribed
input-energy budget. A finite transmitted-photon budget makes these
rates sensitive to both the channel noise and the energy supplied by the
sender. Noise photons at the receiver do not relax this input constraint.

The PLOB bounds provide energy-independent upper bounds for these three
channel families~\cite{PLOB}. Gaussian squashed-entanglement constructions
give energy-dependent bounds~\cite{DSW,Goodenough}, while coherent and reverse
coherent information yield explicit achievable rates
\cite{DevetakWinter,PGPBL,RCI}. More elaborate key-generation and
entanglement-distillation protocols can improve the lower bounds
\cite{OLCSPB,OPB,MLG,BPinterpolation,BPdistillation,BPthermal}.
For noisy Gaussian channels, these approaches generally leave a gap between
achievable rates and converse bounds.

Reference~\cite{LossAmplifier} determines the energy-constrained
two-way capacities of the pure-loss and quantum-limited amplifier channels
using \emph{sector teleportation simulation}. This method encodes the
transmitted photon number in an exact simulation resource and accommodates
the amplifier's unbounded output photon number. We extend that framework
to positive excess noise, reusing the simulation, truncation, and
operational testing tools of the same reference. The channel-dependent
step is the evaluation of the mixed noisy resources.

The main additional challenge is that the simulated resource is itself
noisy. Even after fixing the total input and output photon numbers, the
state retains uncertainty over the different attenuation and amplification
processes that may have occurred. We therefore evaluate its entanglement
directly and show that the resulting quantity has the structure needed for
the converse. In the large-sector limit, this leads to a common
energy-dependent function that applies to attenuation, amplification, and
unit-gain additive noise.

The resulting converse applies to arbitrary correlated adaptive inputs.
A photon-number decomposition introduces only a sublinear correction, which
vanishes asymptotically, while the large-sector entanglement density allows
the bound to be expressed directly in terms of the actual unconditional
transmitted energies. These ingredients produce energy-dependent converse
bounds that improve the previously available bounds and, for the pure-loss
and quantum-limited amplifier channels, meet the corresponding achievable
rates.
More broadly, the results show that retaining the physical input-energy
budget within channel simulation can turn otherwise energy-insensitive
entanglement bounds into sharp finite-energy converses for adaptive bosonic
communication.

Section~\ref{sec:results} presents the main results and comparisons, and
Sec.~\ref{sec:conclusion} gives the conclusions.
Appendix~\ref{app:tools} organizes the inherited tools around the
operational target, sector simulation, and photon-number accounting;
Appendix~\ref{app:resource} evaluates the corresponding resource states,
Appendix~\ref{app:converse} proves the common adaptive weak converse, and
Appendix~\ref{app:benchmarks} treats limits and benchmarks.

\section{Main results}\label{sec:results}
\subsection{Channels and operational assumptions}\label{sec:model}
We use quadratures for which the vacuum covariance is $I_2:=\mathrm{diag}(1,1)$. A centered,
phase-insensitive Gaussian channel $\cG_{\tau,v}$ is specified by
\begin{equation}
 \bar r\longmapsto\sqrt\tau\,\bar r,\qquad
 V\longmapsto\tau V+(2v+1-\tau)I_2.
 \label{eq:channel}
\end{equation}
Here $\tau>0$ is the intensity gain and $v$ is the output mean photon
number for vacuum input. Complete positivity requires
$v\ge\max\{0,\tau-1\}$. We consider the strictly noisy region
\begin{equation}
 v>\max\{0,\tau-1\},\qquad
 \Tr[\hat n\,\cG_{\tau,v}(\rho)]=\tau\Tr(\hat n\rho)+v.
 \label{eq:mean-output}
\end{equation}
Table~\ref{tab:channels} identifies the three families. For thermal loss
and noisy amplification, $\nu>0$ is the environmental thermal photon
number. For additive noise our convention is
\begin{equation}
 \cN_\xi(\rho)=\int_{\mathbb C}\frac{\dd^2\alpha}{\pi\xi}
 e^{-|\alpha|^2/\xi}D(\alpha)\rho D(\alpha)^\dagger,
 \qquad\xi>0,
 \label{eq:additive-definition}
\end{equation}
so $\xi$ is the number of photons added to a vacuum input.
These channels are entanglement breaking precisely when $v\ge\tau$
\cite{HolevoWerner,PLOB,MLG}.

\begin{table}[tb]
\caption{Channel parameters in Eq.~\eqref{eq:channel}. The thermal
environmental photon number is $\nu>0$ in the first two rows.}
\label{tab:channels}
\begin{ruledtabular}
\begin{tabular}{lcc}
Channel & $\tau$ & $v$\\
\hline
Thermal loss $\cL_{\eta,\nu}$ & $0<\eta<1$ & $(1-\eta)\nu$\\
Noisy amplifier $\cA_{\kappa,\nu}$ & $\kappa>1$ & $(\kappa-1)(\nu+1)$\\
Additive noise $\cN_\xi$ & $1$ & $\xi>0$
\end{tabular}
\end{ruledtabular}
\end{table}

Alice and Bob start from local preparations and public randomness,
without shared entanglement or secret key. Between uses of the memoryless
channel they perform adaptive local operations and authenticated two-way
public classical communication (LOCC). Each finite protocol has finitely
many local rounds and finite or countable outcomes; its round bound may
grow with the number of channel uses. States and instruments are normal
on separable Hilbert spaces. Local memories and apparatus have no
dimension, energy, or entropy constraint. Eve holds the channel
environments, their purifications, and every public message. These are
the operational assumptions defining the energy-constrained capacities
studied here, as in Ref.~\cite[Appendix A 2]{LossAmplifier}.

For an $n$-use protocol, constrain only the actual unconditional
transmitted means:
\begin{equation}
 x_i=\Tr(\hat n_{A_i}\rho^{(i)}_{A_i}),\qquad
 \sum_{i=1}^n x_i\le nN<\infty.
 \label{eq:energy}
\end{equation}
This average includes unsuccessful branches and all random choices and
public outcomes. Every channel slot counts toward the rate. For a fixed
channel $\cG$ and budget $N$, the energy-constrained capacities
$Q_2,D_2,P_2,K_2$ concern quantum transmission, entanglement distribution,
private classical transmission, and secret-key agreement, respectively.
Each is the supremum of achievable rates $\liminf_n n^{-1}\log_2d_n$
over protocol sequences obeying Eq.~\eqref{eq:energy} with vanishing
error and finite target size $d_n$ at every blocklength. We suppress
the arguments $(\cG,N)$ when they are clear. The standard task
conversions preserve all optical inputs and give
\cite[Appendix A 2]{LossAmplifier}
\begin{equation}
 Q_2=D_2\le P_2=K_2.
 \label{eq:operational-equivalences}
\end{equation}
Quantum error is entanglement infidelity $\varepsilon$. Key error is
\begin{equation}
 e=\tfrac12\norm{\kappa_{K_AK_BE_{\rm all}}
       -\tau_d^{\rm key}\otimes\kappa_{E_{\rm all}}}_1,
 \label{eq:key-error}
\end{equation}
where $\tau_d^{\rm key}=d^{-1}\sum_{a=0}^{d-1}\proj{aa}$, and the ideal
comparison retains Eve's actual marginal and full transcript
\cite{PortmannRenner}. Private communication uses the analogous criterion
for a uniform message.

\subsection{A common energy-constrained weak converse}\label{sec:unified}
Let us define the entropic function
\begin{equation}
 g(x)=(x+1)\log_2(x+1)-x\log_2x,~~
 \varphi(x)=x\log_2x,
 \label{eq:entropy-functions}
\end{equation}
with $g(0)=\varphi(0)=0$. For $N\ge0$, put
\begin{equation}
 \begin{aligned}
 y&=\tau N+v,\qquad L=1+N+y,\\
 t&=\frac{L-\sqrt{L^2-4\tau N(N+1)}}2,\\
 u&=N-t,\qquad w=y-t.
 \end{aligned}
 \label{eq:rateparams}
\end{equation}
The parameters $u,w$ are nonnegative. In the non-entanglement-breaking
region $v<\tau$, define
\begin{equation}
 \boxed{\begin{aligned}
 \cF_{\tau,v}(N)={}&\varphi(t)+\varphi(L-t)-\varphi(N)\\
 &-\varphi(y)-g(u)-g(w).
 \end{aligned}}
 \label{eq:F}
\end{equation}
For $v\ge\tau$, set $\cF_{\tau,v}(N)=0$.

\begin{theorem}[Energy-constrained weak converse]\label{thm:gaussian}
For every channel in Table~\ref{tab:channels}, under the operational
assumptions and energy constraint above, we have
\begin{equation}
 \boxed{\begin{aligned}
 Q_2(\cG_{\tau,v},N)&=D_2(\cG_{\tau,v},N)\\
 &\le P_2(\cG_{\tau,v},N)=K_2(\cG_{\tau,v},N)\\
 &\le\cF_{\tau,v}(N).
 \end{aligned}}
 \label{eq:capacities}
\end{equation}
\end{theorem}
\noindent Note that the function $\cF_{\tau,v}$ is nonnegative, nondecreasing, and concave
on $[0,\infty)$, with $\cF_{\tau,v}(0)=0$. It is at most $g(N)$ and
approaches the corresponding PLOB bound for large $N$.

The statement applies to arbitrary adaptive inputs with quantum
memories. It neither restricts the protocol to Gaussian operations nor
asserts equality between the quantum and key capacities. The
energy-constrained capacities of the two quantum-limited boundary
channels are determined in Ref.~\cite{LossAmplifier}; the present
theorem specifically concerns positive excess noise.

