\section{Reduction to the noisy-resource calculation}\label{app:tools}
This appendix traces the converse from its operational target to the
sector resource and the cost of resolving photon number. Each step
identifies the needed result from the common pure-loss and
quantum-limited amplifier analysis in Ref.~\cite{LossAmplifier},
using the protocol and error conventions of Sec.~\ref{sec:model}.
Its Appendices A--D provide the shared tools, and Appendix E treats
the amplifier's unbounded output.

\subsection{The entanglement required at the protocol output}\label{app:tests}\label{app:ree}
The quantity propagated through the proof is regularized relative
entropy of entanglement (REE):
\begin{equation}
 \ER(A:B)_\rho=\inf_{\sigma\in\SEP(A:B)}D(\rho\Vert\sigma),
 \qquad
 \ERi(\rho)=\lim_{r\to\infty}\frac{\ER(\rho^{\otimes r})}{r}.
 \label{eq:ree}
\end{equation}
Here $\SEP$ is the trace-norm closed convex hull of product states;
relative entropy has its usual support and extended-value conventions.
For finite $\ER(\rho)$, the limit exists by subadditivity
\cite{Vedral1997,VedralPlenio1998}. We use the normal-state interpretation
recalled in Ref.~\cite[Appendix A 3]{LossAmplifier}.

The operational input to our converse is the following target-test
bound, established for entanglement in
Ref.~\cite[Appendix C 2]{LossAmplifier} and for private states
with unrestricted shields in Ref.~\cite[Appendix D 2]{LossAmplifier}.
If a retained state $\zeta$ with finite REE passes a size-$d$ target
test with failure probability at most $\delta$, then
\begin{equation}
 \ERi(\zeta)\ge(1-\delta)\log_2d-h_2(\delta).
 \label{eq:test-bound}
\end{equation}
The separable acceptance bound $1/d$ extends to $d^{-r}$ for products
of $r$ tests, including correlated separable comparisons. The cited
threshold-test argument converts this fact into the regularized bound.

For a quantum target take $\delta=\varepsilon$. For a key protocol,
the input-preserving retained implementation of
Ref.~\cite[Appendix D 3]{LossAmplifier} keeps local
purifications and Kraus registers and supplies a private-state test
with acceptance at least $(1-e)^2$~\cite{HHHO}. Thus
$\delta=2e-e^2$ suffices. Subsequent operations ignore the extra local
registers, so every optical marginal and every unconditional mean
$x_i$ are preserved. Public records still reach Eve; the channel
environment and its thermal purification remain in $E_{\rm all}$.
Thus the retained-state bound uses the original energy budget and
requires no shield-dimension assumption.

\subsection{The resource supplied at a fixed transmitted photon number}\label{app:simulation}
For $s$ transmitted modes with total photon number $m$, define
\begin{equation}
 \cH_{s,m}=\operatorname{span}\{\ket{\bm r}:|\bm r|=m\},\qquad
 d_{s,m}=\binom{s+m-1}{m},\qquad
 \ket{\Phi_{s,m}}=\frac{1}{\sqrt{d_{s,m}}}
       \sum_{|\bm r|=m}\ket{\bm r}_R\ket{\bm r}_{A^{\prime s}}.
 \label{eq:sector}
\end{equation}
A pure-state symbol without a ket denotes its projector. For
$\cG=\cG_{\tau,v}$ the supplied resource and its output-number
resolution are
\begin{equation}
 \mathcal J_{s,m}=(\id_R\otimes\cG^{\otimes s})(\Phi_{s,m}),
 \qquad
 \mathcal J_{s,m}=\bigoplus_{k\ge0}p_k\rho_{s,m,k}.
 \label{eq:J}
\end{equation}
Alice's resource share has exactly $m$ photons, while Bob's mean is
$\tau m+sv$. The auxiliary input $A^{\prime s}$ defines this state;
it is not an additional transmission by the protocol.

The simulator is imported from Ref.~\cite[Appendix B 1]{LossAmplifier},
with its full Fock-space output implementation in
Ref.~\cite[Appendix E 1]{LossAmplifier}. The underlying
irreducible-covariance construction is given in Ref.~\cite{GBW}.
To check its hypotheses here, $W_m(U)=\Sym^m U$ acts irreducibly
on $\cH_{s,m}$ and each noisy channel obeys passive covariance:
\begin{equation}
 \cG^{\otimes s}(W_m(U)TW_m(U)^\dagger)
   =\Gamma(U)\cG^{\otimes s}(T)\Gamma(U)^\dagger,
 \qquad \Gamma(U)=\bigoplus_{k\ge0}\Sym^k U.
 \label{eq:covariance}
\end{equation}
Identical thermal environments, and the isotropic additive-displacement
distribution, are invariant under passive rotations. The same covariance
also follows from the two factors in Eq.~\eqref{eq:decomposition}.
Although the output contains all number sectors, $\Gamma(U)$ is a
bounded unitary. The cited finite-outcome LOCC simulator therefore gives
\begin{equation}
 \mathcal T_{s,m}(\rho_{XA^sY}\otimes\mathcal J_{s,m})
   =(\id_X\otimes\cG^{\otimes s}\otimes\id_Y)(\rho_{XA^sY})
 \label{eq:simulation}
\end{equation}
for every input supported on $\cH_{s,m}$, including arbitrary
correlations with memories $X,Y$ on separable Hilbert spaces.
LOCC monotonicity and subadditivity then make
$f_s(m)=\ER(\mathcal J_{s,m})$ an upper bound on the entanglement
increase for that sector. Evaluating this noisy resource is the task
of Appendix~\ref{app:resource}.

\subsection{Accounting for photon-number resolution}\label{app:counts}
For inputs spanning several sectors, selective monotonicity and the
local-flags identity give
\begin{equation}
 \sum_jp_j\ER(\rho_j)\le\ER(\rho),\qquad
 \ER\!\left(\bigoplus_jp_j\rho_j\right)=\sum_jp_j\ER(\rho_j).
 \label{eq:flags}
\end{equation}
The first formula applies to a local instrument acting on $\rho$;
the second to locally distinguishable branches. Their countable-outcome
forms are recalled in Ref.~\cite[Appendix A 3]{LossAmplifier}.

For a nonnegative integer count $K$ of mean $z$, and for
a finite-dimensional state $\omega$ pinched in local photon number,
\begin{equation}
 H(K)\le g(z),\qquad
 \ER(\omega)\le\ER(\cP_B\omega)+H(K).
 \label{eq:pinching}
\end{equation}
Here $K$ in the second inequality is the pinching outcome. The
geometric entropy bound and this coherence estimate are proved in
Ref.~\cite[Appendices A 4 and A 5]{LossAmplifier}. Resolving one
total output count of mean $s(\tau x+v)$ therefore costs at most
$g(s(\tau x+v))=o(s)$ at fixed $x$. This term controls the removed
coherence without assuming that photon-number measurement can be undone.

Appendix~\ref{app:block} applies these facts with the output-first
cutoffs of Ref.~\cite[Appendix E 4]{LossAmplifier}.
Their removal uses lower semicontinuity of unregularized REE
\cite[Corollary 20]{LamiShirokov}; replicas then remove the count-entropy
overhead in Appendix~\ref{app:replicas}. The noisy resource density
and its concavity are proved in Appendices~\ref{app:resource}
and~\ref{app:concavity}.

\section{Entanglement of noisy photon-sector resources}\label{app:resource}
Throughout this appendix,
$\max\{0,\tau-1\}<v<\tau$. The same algebra applies on both sides
of unit gain. Use the decomposition
\begin{equation}
 G=1+v,\qquad a=\frac{\tau}{G},\qquad
 \cG_{\tau,v}=\cA_{G,0}\circ\cL_a,\qquad 0<a<1<G.
 \label{eq:decomposition}
\end{equation}
This factorization is used to calculate the noisy resource. No flag
distinguishing its internal loss and amplification histories is supplied
to either party. The only resolved output label is the actual total
photon number $k$. The comparison parameter is
\begin{equation}
 z=\frac{a}{(1-a)v}
   =\frac{\tau}{(1+v-\tau)v}>1,
 \qquad z>1\ \Longleftrightarrow\ v<\tau.
 \label{eq:witness-domain}
\end{equation}
In particular, it is $\kappa/[(\kappa-1)^2\nu(\nu+1)]$ for noisy
amplification and $1/\xi^2$ for additive noise. The positivity used
below therefore holds throughout the non-entanglement-breaking region
of each family.

We use the condensate comparison from
Ref.~\cite[Appendices B 2 and E 2]{LossAmplifier} as the
starting separable state. For a unit vector $u\in\mathbb C^s$, let
$\ket{u;r}$ be the normalized condensate associated with $u^{\otimes r}$,
and put
\begin{equation}
 \sigma_{s,m,k}=\int\proj{\bar u;m}_R\otimes\proj{u;k}_B\,\dd u,
 \label{eq:sigma}
\end{equation}
where $\dd u$ is normalized uniform measure on unit vectors. The
optimality and product additivity of this comparison for the mixed
noisy resources are established below.

\subsection{Optimal conditional comparison and product additivity}
Recall $d_{s,r}=\binom{s+r-1}{r}$ and set $d_{s,-1}=0$.
In the noisy case, conditioning on $m$ and $k$ still leaves a
mixture of internal histories. The lemma evaluates its REE directly
and gives the product additivity used in Appendix~\ref{app:concavity}.
\begin{lemma}[Conditional comparison and additivity]\label{lem:optimal}
For $s\ge2$, the separable state $\sigma_{s,m,k}$ in \eqref{eq:sigma},
now defined for every $k\ge0$, satisfies
\begin{equation}
 \begin{aligned}
 \ER(\rho_{s,m,k})&=D(\rho_{s,m,k}\Vert\sigma_{s,m,k}),\\
 \ER(\mathcal J_{s,m})&=\sum_k p_kD(\rho_{s,m,k}\Vert\sigma_{s,m,k}).
 \end{aligned}
 \label{eq:res-exactJ}
\end{equation}
REE is additive on arbitrary finite products of these conditional
states and of the resources $\mathcal J_{s,m}$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
\emph{Spectrum.} Put $C_-=\sum_{i=1}^s r_i b_i$, $C_+=C_-^\dagger$,
where $r_i,b_i$ annihilate reference and output photons. The commutator
$[C_-,C_+]=s+\hat N_R+\hat N_B$ decomposes the fixed $(m,k)$ space into
orthogonal blocks $\Pi_h$: apply $C_+^h$ to the kernel of $C_-$ at
$(m-h,k-h)$, for $0\le h\le\min(m,k)$. With $A=s+m+k-1$,
\begin{align}
 D_h:=\dim\Pi_h
 &=d_{s,m-h}d_{s,k-h}-d_{s,m-h-1}d_{s,k-h-1},\label{eq:res-dim}\\
 C_+^jC_-^j\big|_{\Pi_h}&=\fall hj\fall{(A-h)}j I.
 \label{eq:res-ladder}
\end{align}
Here $\fall nj=n(n-1)\cdots(n-j+1)$ and $\fall n0=1$.
The dimension formula follows because $C_-C_+$ is strictly positive,
so $C_-$ is onto the preceding number space; the ladder eigenvalues
then establish orthogonality and completeness.
More explicitly, on a lowest-weight vector $\ket\xi$ with numbers
$(m_0,k_0)$ and $C_-\ket\xi=0$, the commutator gives
\begin{equation}
 C_-C_+^h\ket\xi
 =h(s+m_0+k_0+h-1)C_+^{h-1}\ket\xi.
\end{equation}
Putting $m_0=m-h$, $k_0=k-h$ and iterating proves
\eqref{eq:res-ladder}. The eigenvalues of $C_+C_-$ are $h(A-h)$;
their successive differences are $A-2h-1>0$ on the allowed range.
The blocks are therefore orthogonal, and the dimensions in
\eqref{eq:res-dim} telescope to $d_{s,m}d_{s,k}$, exhausting the space.

Set $z=a/[(1-a)v]>1$. The eigenvalues of $p_k\rho_{s,m,k}$ and
$\sigma_{s,m,k}$ on $\Pi_h$ are, respectively,
\begin{align}
 \lambda_{k,h}&=\frac{(1-a)^m v^k}{d_{s,m}G^{s+k}}
 H_h(z),\label{eq:res-eigen}\\
 H_h(z)&=\sum_{j=0}^h\binom hj\binom{A-h}{j}z^j,\\
 \mu_h&=\frac{m!k!(s-1)!}{h!(A-h)!}>0.
 \label{eq:res-mu}
\end{align}
To obtain the first formula, choose $0<b<1$ and send half of
$\sqrt{1-b}\sum_{n\ge0}b^{n/2}\ket{n,n}$ through the channel and take
$s$ copies. The output is
\begin{equation}
 \left(\frac{1-b}{G}\right)^s
 e^{\sqrt{ab/G}\,C_+}[b(1-a)]^{\hat N_R}(v/G)^{\hat N_B}
 e^{\sqrt{ab/G}\,C_-}.
 \label{eq:res-normal}
\end{equation}
Its coherent-state matrix elements give the normalized Husimi Gaussian
with means $b/(1-b)$ and $\tau b/(1-b)+v$, and correlation
$\sqrt{\tau b}/(1-b)$. To check normalization and identify the state,
write $q_R=b(1-a)$, $q_B=v/G$, and $\chi=\sqrt{ab/G}$.
For one pair the Husimi density is
\begin{equation}
 \frac{1-b}{\pi^2G}
 \exp\!\left[-(1-q_R)|\alpha|^2-(1-q_B)|\beta|^2
                   +2\chi\operatorname{Re}(\alpha\beta)\right].
\end{equation}
Its precision determinant is
$(1-q_R)(1-q_B)-\chi^2=(1-b)/G>0$. Inverting that matrix gives
diagonal moments $N+1$ and $\tau N+v+1$ and correlation
$\sqrt{\tau N(N+1)}$, where $N=b/(1-b)$; the extra ones are the
heterodyne vacuum noise. This identifies the required Gaussian output.

Projection onto Alice's number $m$ has probability
$d_{s,m}(1-b)^s b^m$. Projecting also onto Bob's number $k$ leaves
equal powers from the two exponential series. After dividing by
Alice's probability, one obtains the operator identity
\begin{equation}
 p_k\rho_{s,m,k}
 =\frac{(1-a)^m v^k}{d_{s,m}G^{s+k}}
   \sum_{j=0}^{\min(m,k)}\frac{z^j}{(j!)^2}C_+^jC_-^j.
 \label{eq:res-resource-polynomial}
\end{equation}
Equation~\eqref{eq:res-ladder} now gives \eqref{eq:res-eigen} directly.
For \eqref{eq:res-mu}, the heterodyne measure-and-prepare channel
$\alpha\mapsto\ket{\sqrt c\,\alpha}$, $c>0$, has $G=1+c$, $a=c/(1+c)$ and
$z=1$ \cite{MLG}. Its conditional resource is precisely the condensate
twirl \eqref{eq:sigma}. Hence Vandermonde's identity
$H_h(1)=\binom Ah$, followed by normalization, gives \eqref{eq:res-mu}.
For completeness, the measure-and-prepare map is
$T\mapsto\int\pi^{-s}\dd^{2s}\alpha\,
\langle\alpha|T|\alpha\rangle\proj{\sqrt c\,\alpha}$.
After fixing $(m,k)$, its radial integral is a scalar and its angular
integral is exactly \eqref{eq:sigma}. The corresponding output-count
probability is
\begin{equation}
 p_k^{\rm het}=\binom{s+m+k-1}{k}
              \frac{c^k}{(1+c)^{s+m+k}}.
\end{equation}
Dividing the eigenvalue in \eqref{eq:res-eigen} at $z=1$ by this
probability gives the stated $\mu_h$. Every $\mu_h$ is positive, so
the comparison and its inverse have full support on the finite
$(m,k)$ space.

\emph{Optimality and products.} The comparison optimization may be
restricted to the local $(m,k)$ subspace. Indeed, locally pinching any
separable comparison into that subspace and its complement cannot
increase its relative entropy to $\rho_{s,m,k}$. If its weight in the
subspace is $w>0$, normalizing that block removes the nonnegative
penalty $-\log_2w$ and preserves separability. A zero-weight block
gives infinite relative entropy. We therefore optimize on the finite
$(m,k)$ space throughout.

Expanding $H_h$ about $z=1$ and applying
Vandermonde's identity gives
\begin{equation}
 \frac{H_h(z)}{\binom Ah}
 =\sum_{j=0}^h\frac{\fall hj\fall{(A-h)}j}{j!\fall Aj}(z-1)^j.
\end{equation}
Consequently, for some normalization $c_{m,k}>0$,
\begin{equation}
 W:=\rho_{s,m,k}\sigma_{s,m,k}^{-1}
 =c_{m,k}\sum_{j=0}^{\min(m,k)}
       \frac{(z-1)^j}{j!\fall Aj}C_+^jC_-^j.
 \label{eq:res-witness}
\end{equation}
Every coefficient is nonnegative. For a product state $\alpha_R\otimes\beta_B$,
the symmetric-tensor representation gives
\begin{equation}
 \Tr[(\alpha\otimes\beta)C_+^jC_-^j]
 =\fall mj\fall kj\,\Tr[(\alpha^{(j)})^T\beta^{(j)}]
 \le\fall mj\fall kj,
 \label{eq:res-contractbound}
\end{equation}
where the superscripts denote normalized particle reductions.
The transpose is in the induced tensor basis. The reduced operators
are density operators, whose overlap is at most one; the falling
factorials count the ordered annihilation strings.
Aligned conjugate condensates attain every inequality. Their twirl is
$\sigma$, so $\max_{\tau\in\mathrm{SEP}}\Tr W\tau=\Tr W\sigma=1$.
Since $\rho$ and $\sigma$ commute, the directional derivative of
$D(\rho\Vert\sigma)$ towards any separable $\tau$ is
$(1-\Tr W\tau)/\ln2\ge0$. Convexity proves optimality
\cite{VollbrechtWerner}.

For $r$ blocks, let $\alpha_{R_1\cdots R_r}$ and
$\beta_{B_1\cdots B_r}$ be arbitrary local states, possibly entangled
between blocks within each laboratory. For
$\boldsymbol j=(j_1,\ldots,j_r)$, denote by
$\alpha^{(\boldsymbol j)},\beta^{(\boldsymbol j)}$ their normalized
joint particle reductions, retaining $j_\ell$ particles in block $\ell$.
Expanding the contractions gives
\begin{align}
 &\Tr\!\left[(\alpha\otimes\beta)
       \bigotimes_{\ell=1}^r C_{\ell,+}^{j_\ell}C_{\ell,-}^{j_\ell}\right]
 \notag\\
 &\quad=\left(\prod_{\ell=1}^r
                 \fall{m_\ell}{j_\ell}\fall{k_\ell}{j_\ell}\right)
       \Tr[(\alpha^{(\boldsymbol j)})^T\beta^{(\boldsymbol j)}]
 \le\prod_{\ell=1}^r\fall{m_\ell}{j_\ell}\fall{k_\ell}{j_\ell}.
 \label{eq:res-joint-reduction}
\end{align}
The reductions need not factor between blocks. All coefficients in
the product expansion of \eqref{eq:res-witness} are nonnegative, and
aligned condensates saturate every term simultaneously. Therefore
$\Tr[(\bigotimes_\ell W_\ell)\tau]\le1$ for every state separable
across the aggregate Alice--Bob cut. The same directional-derivative
argument proves optimality of the product comparison. Additivity of
relative entropy on that comparison proves the claimed conditional
REE additivity, without assuming general REE additivity.

Finally, for the countable output flags, the separable comparison
$\Sigma=\bigoplus_{k\ge0}p_k\sigma_{s,m,k}$ gives
\begin{equation}
 \ER(\mathcal J_{s,m})\le
 D(\mathcal J_{s,m}\Vert\Sigma)
 =\sum_{k\ge0}p_kD(\rho_{s,m,k}\Vert\sigma_{s,m,k}).
\end{equation}
For the reverse inequality, Bob resolves the finitely many outcomes
$k\le K_0$ and one combined tail outcome. Strong monotonicity bounds
the resource REE below by the partial sum up to $K_0$; letting
$K_0\to\infty$ proves equality. Each conditional cost is at most
$\log_2d_{s,m}$, so the full average is finite. Applying the same
finite-flag truncation to each of finitely many resources, together
with \eqref{eq:res-joint-reduction}, proves their product additivity.
For $s=1$, the reference sector is one-dimensional and all these
resources are separable, so the corresponding claims are immediate.
\end{proof}

\subsection{Asymptotic entanglement per mode}
\begin{lemma}[Sector entanglement density]\label{lem:density}
For every sequence $m_s/s\to N\ge0$,
\begin{equation}
 \frac{\ER(\mathcal J_{s,m_s})}{s}\longrightarrow\cF_{\tau,v}(N),
 \label{eq:res-density}
\end{equation}
where $\cF_{\tau,v}$ is the function in \eqref{eq:F}.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Suppose $N>0$ and put $N_s=m_s/s$. Let $\omega_{N_s}$ be the noisy-channel
output of a two-mode squeezed vacuum of input mean $N_s$. In
$\omega_{N_s}^{\otimes s}$ let $M$ be Alice's total photon number.
Conditioning on $M=m_s$ gives $\mathcal J_{s,m_s}$, with probability
\begin{equation}
 \begin{aligned}
 \Pr(M=m_s)&=d_{s,m_s}\frac{N_s^{m_s}}{(1+N_s)^{s+m_s}}\\
 &\sim[2\pi sN_s(1+N_s)]^{-1/2}.
 \end{aligned}
 \label{eq:res-conditioning-probability}
\end{equation}
This is Stirling's formula at the exact mean $sN_s=m_s$; the
polynomial conditioning probability is essential to the following step.

Before conditioning, Bob's number $K$ is the sum of $s$ independent
thermal counts of mean $y_s=\tau N_s+v$, and hence
$\mathbb E(K/s-y_s)^2=y_s(1+y_s)/s=O(s^{-1})$.
For the other observable set
$c_s=\sqrt{\tau N_s(N_s+1)}$, $Z_i=r_i b_i-c_s$, and $Y_s=\sum_iZ_i$.
Then
\begin{equation}
 C_+C_- -s^2c_s^2=s c_s(Y_s+Y_s^\dagger)+Y_s^\dagger Y_s.
 \label{eq:res-centered-ladder}
\end{equation}
Centered independence gives
$\langle(Y_s+Y_s^\dagger)^2\rangle=O(s)$ and
$\langle(Y_s^\dagger Y_s)^2\rangle=O(s^2)$.
For the latter, expand the four indices: any index occurring only once
has zero centered expectation, so only $O(s^2)$ patterns survive.
Their moments are uniformly bounded for $N_s$ near the fixed $N$.
The inequality $(A+B)^2\le2A^2+2B^2$ for self-adjoint $A,B$, applied
to \eqref{eq:res-centered-ladder}, therefore yields
\begin{equation}
 \left\langle\left(C_+C_-/s^2-c_s^2\right)^2\right\rangle=O(s^{-1}).
\end{equation}
Both $K$ and $C_+C_-$ commute with $M$. For either fixed-tolerance
deviation event $D_s$, Chebyshev's inequality and
\eqref{eq:res-conditioning-probability} give
\begin{equation}
 \Pr(D_s\mid M=m_s)
 \le\frac{\Pr(D_s)}{\Pr(M=m_s)}
 =O(s^{-1/2})\longrightarrow0.
 \label{eq:res-conditional-concentration}
\end{equation}
Thus concentration survives this particular conditioning. Under the
conditioned resource, the spectral identity is
$C_+C_-=H(s+m_s+K-1-H)$, with $H$ the label in
\eqref{eq:res-ladder}. Consequently,
\begin{equation}
 K/s\longrightarrow y,\qquad H/s\longrightarrow t
 \quad\hbox{in probability},
 \label{eq:res-concentration}
\end{equation}
where $t(1+N+y-t)=\tau N(N+1)$ has a unique solution in
$[0,\min(N,y)]$. These are exactly $y,t$ in \eqref{eq:rateparams}.
The root is unique because the derivative of $h(1+N+y-h)$ on that
interval is at least $1+|N-y|>0$. This also selects the smaller root
appearing in the main result.

The difference of dimensions in \eqref{eq:res-dim} needs some care.
For $p=m_s-h$ and $r=k-h$, factor it as
\begin{equation}
 D_h=d_{s,p}d_{s,r}
 \left[1-\frac{pr}{(s+p-1)(s+r-1)}\right].
 \label{eq:res-dimension-factor}
\end{equation}
Near the typical values, the bracket tends to
$1-uw/[(1+u)(1+w)]>0$, where $u=N-t$, $w=y-t$, and $L=1+N+y$.
Its logarithm divided by $s$ vanishes, so the subtraction does not
cancel the exponential growth of the two dimensions. Stirling's
formula and \eqref{eq:res-concentration} now give, in probability,
\begin{align}
 s^{-1}\log_2D_H&\longrightarrow g(u)+g(w),\\
 -s^{-1}\log_2\mu_H
 &\longrightarrow\varphi(t)+\varphi(L-t)-\varphi(N)-\varphi(y).
\end{align}

To pass to expectations, first note that
$\mu_{h+1}/\mu_h=(A-h)/(h+1)\ge1$ and
$\mu_0^{-1}=d_{s,m_s}d_{s+m_s,k}$. Together with
$D_h\le d_{s,m_s}d_{s,k}$, these give
\begin{align}
 0\le s^{-1}\log_2D_H&\le g(N_s)+g(K/s),\\
 0\le-s^{-1}\log_2\mu_H
 &\le g(N_s)+(1+N_s)g\!\left(\frac{K/s}{1+N_s}\right).
\end{align}
Here $\sup_s\mathbb E[K/s]<\infty$, since
$\mathbb E[K/s]=\tau N_s+v$. For any nonnegative $X_s$ with bounded
means, the sublinear growth of $g$ gives
\begin{equation}
 \mathbb E[g(X_s)\mathbf1_{\{X_s>R\}}]
 \le\left(\sup_{x>R}\frac{g(x)}x\right)
       \sup_s\mathbb E X_s\longrightarrow0.
 \label{eq:res-uniform-integrability}
\end{equation}
The scaled function in the second bound satisfies the same estimate
uniformly because $N_s$ is bounded. Boundedness on $X_s\le R$ then
proves uniform integrability of both quantities and justifies their
expectation limits.

Under the spectral probability law $q_{k,h}=D_h\lambda_{k,h}$,
\begin{align}
 S(\mathcal J_{s,m_s})&=H(K,H)+\mathbb E\log_2D_H,\\
 \ER(\mathcal J_{s,m_s})
 &=-S(\mathcal J_{s,m_s})+H(K)-\mathbb E\log_2\mu_H.
\end{align}
The second identity uses the optimal flagged comparison in
Lemma~\ref{lem:optimal}. The label entropy is bounded by
$H(K,H)\le g(\tau m_s+sv)+\log_2(m_s+1)=O(\log s)$.
We have therefore proved
\begin{align}
 S(\mathcal J_{s,m_s})/s&\longrightarrow g(u)+g(w),\notag\\
 -\mathbb E\log_2\mu_H/s
 &\longrightarrow\varphi(t)+\varphi(L-t)-\varphi(N)-\varphi(y).
 \label{eq:res-density-terms}
\end{align}
Substituting these limits gives \eqref{eq:F}. For $N=0$, use
$0\le\ER(\mathcal J_{s,m_s})/s\le g(m_s/s)\to0$.
\end{proof}

\section{A common adaptive weak-converse proof}\label{app:converse}
Write $\cE=\cG_{\tau,v}$, $\cF=\cF_{\tau,v}$, and
$f_s(m)=\ER(\mathcal J_{s,m})$, with
$\max\{0,\tau-1\}<v<\tau$. The new resource-density lemma replaces
the quantum-limited cost estimates in
Ref.~\cite[Appendices B 3 and E 3]{LossAmplifier}. We first obtain a
block inequality, remove its count-entropy overhead with replicas,
prove concavity, and then telescope along an adaptive protocol. This
order avoids assuming concavity or a bound on a single noisy sector
before either has been established.

\subsection{A block inequality for an arbitrary correlated input}\label{app:block}
\begin{lemma}[Noisy-channel block entanglement bound]\label{lem:block}
Let $\rho_{XA^sY}$ have finite $\ER(XA^s:Y)_\rho$ and total
transmitted mean $sx$. Let $p_m=\Tr(P_m^A\rho)$, and set
$\omega=(\id_X\otimes\cE^{\otimes s}\otimes\id_Y)(\rho)$. Then
\begin{equation}
 \begin{aligned}
 \ER(X:B^sY)_\omega
 \le{}&\ER(XA^s:Y)_\rho+\sum_mp_m f_s(m)\\
     &+g\bigl(s(\tau x+v)\bigr).
 \end{aligned}
 \label{eq:res-block}
\end{equation}
No dimension or entropy constraint is imposed on $X,Y$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
We apply the photon-number reduction from
Ref.~\cite[Appendices B 4 and E 4]{LossAmplifier}, retaining the
actual sector cost $f_s(m)$ rather than a quantum-limited energy
estimate. First assume finite memories and finite optical input and
output cutoffs. Measure Alice's total input number $M$, with conditional
states $\rho_m$, and let $\omega_m=\cE^{\otimes s}(\rho_m)$.
Phase covariance implies that output total-number pinching obeys
\begin{equation}
 \cP_B\omega=\cE^{\otimes s}(\cP_A\rho)
             =\sum_mp_m\omega_m.
 \label{eq:phase-pinching}
\end{equation}
Thus removing coherence between output total numbers removes precisely
the contribution of input coherence between total-number sectors. The
within-sector state can still be arbitrary and correlated with both
memories.

For each sector, exact simulation~\eqref{eq:simulation} and
subadditivity give
\begin{equation}
 \ER(X:B^sY)_{\omega_m}
 \le\ER(XA^s:Y)_{\rho_m}+f_s(m).
 \label{eq:sector-gain}
\end{equation}
The resource is supplied independently conditional on $m$, so the
simulation acts on $\rho_m\otimes\mathcal J_{s,m}$. Convexity and
strong monotonicity now give the complete accounting chain
\begin{align}
 \ER(\cP_B\omega)
 &\le\sum_mp_m\ER(\omega_m)\notag\\
 &\le\sum_mp_m\ER(\rho_m)+\sum_mp_m f_s(m)\notag\\
 &\le\ER(\rho)+\sum_mp_m f_s(m).
 \label{eq:block-chain}
\end{align}
Restoring the coherence in the inequality, rather than by a physical
operation, costs at most $H(K)$ by \eqref{eq:pinching}. Bob's total
output count has mean $s(\tau x+v)$, so
\begin{equation}
 \ER(\omega)\le\ER(\cP_B\omega)+H(K),\qquad
 H(K)\le g\bigl(s(\tau x+v)\bigr).
 \label{eq:block-overhead}
\end{equation}
This proves \eqref{eq:res-block} in finite dimensions. Notice that only
one total output count is pinched. Pinching each mode separately would
generally introduce an extensive entropy overhead.

For the cutoff removal we use the arbitrary-input reduction
of Ref.~\cite[Appendix B 4]{LossAmplifier} in the output-first form
proved in its Appendix E 4. The latter
form handles the unbounded photon number created by noise. Its
application here requires control of the averaged noisy-resource cost,
which we give explicitly. On either optical block use
the local vacuum-replacement channel
\begin{equation}
 \mathcal C_J(T)=P_{\le J}TP_{\le J}
       +\Tr[(I-P_{\le J})T]\proj{0^{\otimes s}}.
 \label{eq:cutoff}
\end{equation}
The output cutoff commutes with phase rotations, decreases the output
mean, and can be appended to the simulator without increasing its
resource cost. Finite-rank memory cutoffs leave the optical marginal
unchanged. The input cutoff preserves $p_m$ for $0<m\le J$ and
transfers the discarded mass to $m=0$. Since $f_s(0)=0$, it cannot
increase the sector-cost average. Moreover,
\begin{equation}
 0\le\sum_m p_mf_s(m)\le\sum_m p_m\log_2d_{s,m}
       \le s\sum_m p_mg(m/s)\le sg(x)<\infty.
 \label{eq:resource-domination}
\end{equation}
First remove the output cutoff at fixed input and memory cutoffs,
then remove the latter cutoffs. The cutoff states converge in trace
norm, and lower semicontinuity of unregularized REE
\cite{LamiShirokov} gives the block inequality with its original
right-hand side. This is precisely the feature of the amplifier
cutoff argument needed for all three noisy channels. No memory
entropy is evaluated and no cutoff limit is exchanged with REE
regularization.
\end{proof}

\subsection{Replicas remove the photon-count overhead}\label{app:replicas}
We use the replica method of
Ref.~\cite[Appendix B 5]{LossAmplifier}, also used for amplification
in its Appendix E 5. The new step is
to average the resource-density limit of Lemma~\ref{lem:density}
over the random input sector using only its first photon-number moment.
\begin{lemma}[One-use regularized entanglement gain]\label{lem:gain}
For an input $\rho_{XAY}$ with $\ER(XA:Y)_\rho<\infty$ and
$x=\Tr(\hat n_A\rho_A)<\infty$, let
$\omega=(\id_X\otimes\cE\otimes\id_Y)(\rho)$. Then
\begin{equation}
 \ERi(X:BY)_\omega
 \le\ERi(XA:Y)_\rho+\cF(x).
 \label{eq:gain-proof}
\end{equation}
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Apply Lemma~\ref{lem:block} to $s$ independent copies of this one
fixed input, treating $A^s$ as the optical block and $X^s,Y^s$ as the
memories. If $M_s$ is the sum of their photon counts, this gives
\begin{equation}
 \begin{aligned}
 \frac{\ER(\omega^{\otimes s})}{s}
 \le{}&\frac{\ER(\rho^{\otimes s})}{s}
       +\EE\frac{f_s(M_s)}s\\
     &+\frac{g(s(\tau x+v))}{s}.
 \end{aligned}
 \label{eq:replica-bound}
\end{equation}
The variables summed in $M_s$ are independent with finite mean $x$;
no finite variance is required. The law of large numbers gives
$M_s/s\to x$ in probability. Lemma~\ref{lem:density} applies to
every deterministic sequence $m_s/s\to x$. It consequently implies
local uniform convergence along these lattice points: a violating
subsequence would itself contradict that lemma. Therefore
$f_s(M_s)/s\to\cF(x)$ in probability.

To justify averaging, use
$0\le f_s(M_s)/s\le g(M_s/s)$ and the first-moment estimate
\begin{equation}
 \EE\bigl[g(M_s/s)\mathbf1_{\{M_s/s>R\}}\bigr]
 \le x\sup_{z>R}\frac{g(z)}z\longrightarrow0.
 \label{eq:replica-ui}
\end{equation}
The right-hand side vanishes because $g(z)=O(\log z)$; the variables
are bounded on $M_s/s\le R$. This proves uniform integrability and
hence
\begin{equation}
 \lim_{s\to\infty}\EE\frac{f_s(M_s)}s=\cF(x).
 \label{eq:averaged-density}
\end{equation}
Finally $g(s(\tau x+v))/s\to0$, while the other terms converge to
the regularized REEs. Output REE is finite already by
Lemma~\ref{lem:block} at $s=1$. Taking $s\to\infty$ in
\eqref{eq:replica-bound} proves \eqref{eq:gain-main}.
\end{proof}

The replica parameter $s$ is an auxiliary proof parameter. It is not
the number of consecutive uses of an adaptive protocol, and the
argument does not assume that successive adaptive inputs are
independent. In a protocol, Lemma~\ref{lem:gain} will be applied to
the actual unconditional input and memories at each fixed channel
use, with the protocol itself held fixed.

\subsection{Why the energy function is concave}\label{app:concavity}
\begin{lemma}[Gaussian-state density and concavity]\label{lem:concavity}
Let $\omega_N$ be the output obtained by sending one arm of a two-mode
squeezed vacuum of mean $N$ through $\cE$. Then
\begin{equation}
 \ERi(\omega_N)=\cF(N).
 \label{eq:gaussian-density}
\end{equation}
The function $\cF$ is concave and nondecreasing.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
The product additivity proved in Lemma~\ref{lem:optimal} is needed
here only for sector resources, not for arbitrary states. For a finite
list of energies $N_i$ and nonnegative integer group sizes $n_i$, set
\begin{equation}
 A(\boldsymbol n)=\sum_{i:n_i>0}\EE f_{n_i}(M_i),
 \label{eq:group-average}
\end{equation}
where $M_i$ is Alice's total number in $n_i$ copies of $\omega_{N_i}$.
Pinching that number in each group leaves products of sector resources
with local flags. Monotonicity, the flags identity, and sector-resource
additivity imply the lower bound in
\begin{equation}
 \begin{aligned}
 A(\boldsymbol n)
 &\le\ER\!\left(\bigotimes_i\omega_{N_i}^{\otimes n_i}\right)\\
 &\le A(\boldsymbol n)+\sum_{i:n_i>0}H(M_i).
 \end{aligned}
 \label{eq:res-group-sandwich}
\end{equation}
For the upper bound, take the direct sum of the optimal product
sector comparisons, with the actual number probabilities. Its
logarithm is block diagonal, so pinching leaves its cross entropy
unchanged, while the entropy increase is at most $\sum_iH(M_i)$.
All terms are finite: the unpinched states have finite Gaussian
entropy, and $H(M_i)\le g(n_iN_i)$. This also justifies the countable
number decomposition.

For one group, divide \eqref{eq:res-group-sandwich} by its size and
use \eqref{eq:averaged-density} and $g(nN)/n\to0$. This proves
\eqref{eq:gaussian-density}. For concavity, let $p_i$ be a finite
probability distribution and retain its flag locally at both parties:
\begin{equation}
 \Omega=\sum_i p_i\proj i_{C_A}\otimes\proj i_{C_B}\otimes\omega_{N_i}.
 \label{eq:flagged-gaussian}
\end{equation}
In $s$ copies, conditioning on these classical flags produces random
group sizes $n_i$ with $n_i/s\to p_i$. Apply the flags identity to
unregularized REE and then average \eqref{eq:res-group-sandwich}.
The overhead is at most $\sum_i g(sN_i)=O(\log s)$, and
$\EE f_{n_i}(M_i)\le n_i g(N_i)$ controls the averaging over group
sizes. Using \eqref{eq:averaged-density} in each group gives
\begin{equation}
 \ERi(\Omega)=\sum_i p_i\cF(N_i).
 \label{eq:res-flagged-density}
\end{equation}
The original flags are classical and already locally available; they
introduce no extra coherence penalty proportional to $sH(p)$.

Alice can prepare the flagged squeezed inputs locally, with initial
REE zero, and send one arm with unconditional mean
$\bar N=\sum_i p_iN_i$. Lemma~\ref{lem:gain} therefore implies
\begin{equation}
 \sum_i p_i\cF(N_i)=\ERi(\Omega)
 \le\cF\!\left(\sum_i p_iN_i\right).
 \label{eq:concavity}
\end{equation}
This is concavity. It has been obtained without assuming concavity of
the explicit formula or general additivity of regularized REE.
Nonnegativity and finiteness follow from the sector-density limit. The bound $f_s(m)\le sg(m/s)$ also gives $\cF(N)\le g(N)$.
A finite concave function nonnegative on $[0,\infty)$ cannot have a
negative secant slope, since concavity would keep subsequent slopes
negative and eventually force the function below zero. Hence $\cF$
is nondecreasing.
\end{proof}

\subsection{From one use to a fully adaptive protocol}\label{app:adaptive}
The adaptive entanglement accounting of
Ref.~\cite[Appendices B 5 and E 5]{LossAmplifier} now applies to
the gain function just established. The steps below verify its
finiteness assumptions and track the unconditional energies.

Fix an admissible protocol with $n$ channel uses. Immediately before
use $i$, let its unconditional state be
$\rho^{(i)}_{X_iA_iY_i}$, where $X_i,Y_i$ include the retained quantum
memories and all local classical history registers. Arbitrary feedback
and correlations between histories and quantum systems are already
contained in this state. Lemma~\ref{lem:gain} gives
\begin{equation}
 \ERi(X_i:B_iY_i)_{\omega^{(i)}}
 \le\ERi(X_iA_i:Y_i)_{\rho^{(i)}}+\cF(x_i).
 \label{eq:one-use-adaptive}
\end{equation}
The subsequent LOCC cannot increase $\ERi$. Starting from a
separable state and telescoping \eqref{eq:one-use-adaptive} gives
\begin{equation}
 \ERi(\zeta_n)\le\sum_{i=1}^n\cF(x_i).
 \label{eq:telescope}
\end{equation}
All required intermediate unregularized REEs are finite. Indeed,
at $s=1$ every fixed-input-number sector is one-dimensional, so
$f_1(m)=0$; Lemma~\ref{lem:block} bounds a one-use increase by
$g(\tau x_i+v)<\infty$. Induction from the initial state verifies
the finiteness assumption of Lemma~\ref{lem:gain} at every use.

Finally, concavity, monotonicity, and \eqref{eq:energy} imply
\begin{equation}
 \frac1n\sum_{i=1}^n\cF(x_i)
 \le\cF\!\left(\frac1n\sum_i x_i\right)\le\cF(N).
 \label{eq:energy-average}
\end{equation}
This proves \eqref{eq:adaptive}. There is no conditioning on
successful branches and no replacement of the actual energy budget
by a branchwise constraint.

\subsection{Conversion to operational rate bounds}\label{app:operational}
The operational reductions are those of
Ref.~\cite[Appendices C 2, D 2, and D 3]{LossAmplifier}, recalled in
Appendix~\ref{app:tests}. For quantum communication or entanglement
distribution, apply \eqref{eq:test-bound} to the final target
registers and use
\eqref{eq:adaptive}. This yields
\eqref{eq:quantum-error}. For a classical key, use the retained
implementation of Appendix~\ref{app:tests}; it preserves every $x_i$
and has privacy-test failure at most $\delta=2e-e^2$. Equations
\eqref{eq:test-bound} and~\eqref{eq:adaptive} then yield
\eqref{eq:key-bound}. Thus Corollary~\ref{cor:finite} holds.

Divide these inequalities by $n$ and let the operational errors vanish
along a sequence of protocols. Since $h_2\le1$, the entropy terms
vanish after division by $n$, and the coefficients of $\log_2d_n/n$
tend to one. With the conversions in
\eqref{eq:operational-equivalences}, this proves the weak-converse
capacity bound in Theorem~\ref{thm:gaussian}. The replica limit was taken for
each fixed protocol before this communication limit.

\section{Channel limits and benchmark calculations}\label{app:benchmarks}
\subsection{Zero energy and the entanglement-breaking boundary}\label{app:limits}
At $N=0$, Eq.~\eqref{eq:rateparams} gives $(t,u,w)=(0,0,v)$,
so $\cF_{\tau,v}(0)=0$. At $v=\tau$, the root equation implies
$uw=t$, and therefore
\begin{equation}
 N=u(1+w),\qquad y=w(1+u),\qquad L-t=(1+u)(1+w).
 \label{eq:eb-identity}
\end{equation}
Substitution in Eq.~\eqref{eq:F} makes its cross-entropy term equal
to $g(u)+g(w)$. The formula thus approaches zero continuously at this
boundary. For $v\ge\tau$ the channel is entanglement breaking
\cite{HolevoWerner,PLOB,MLG}. Such a channel has a measure-and-prepare
realization and cannot increase laboratory entanglement. The retained
privacy-test argument then also bounds the key capacity by zero.
This establishes the entanglement-breaking cases of
Theorem~\ref{thm:gaussian}.

\subsection{Infinite-energy limits for the three noisy families}\label{app:highenergy}
For fixed non-entanglement-breaking channel parameters define
\begin{equation}
 C_N=\varphi(t)+\varphi(L-t)-\varphi(N)-\varphi(y),
 \qquad S_N=g(u)+g(w),\qquad \cF(N)=C_N-S_N.
 \label{eq:cross-and-entropy}
\end{equation}
We use $g(x)=\log_2x+\log_2e+o(1)$ for $x\to\infty$.
All the following limits keep the channel fixed while $N$ grows.

For thermal loss, $\tau=\eta$ and $v=(1-\eta)\nu$, with $0<\eta<1$.
The smaller root expands as
\begin{equation}
 t=\eta N-\eta\nu+O(N^{-1}),\quad
 u=(1-\eta)N+\eta\nu+O(N^{-1}),\quad
 w=\nu+O(N^{-1}).
 \label{eq:loss-root-limit}
\end{equation}
Taylor expansion of $\varphi$ and the thermal entropy gives
\begin{align}
 C_N&=\log_2N+\log_2e-\nu\log_2\eta+o(1),\\
 S_N&=\log_2N+\log_2(1-\eta)+\log_2e+g(\nu)+o(1).
\end{align}
Their difference is Eq.~\eqref{eq:loss-plob}.

For noisy amplification, $\tau=\kappa>1$ and
$v=(\kappa-1)(\nu+1)$. Now
\begin{equation}
 t=N-\nu+O(N^{-1}),\quad u=\nu+O(N^{-1}),\quad
 w=(\kappa-1)(N+1)+\kappa\nu+O(N^{-1}),
 \label{eq:amp-root-limit}
\end{equation}
so
\begin{align}
 C_N&=\log_2N+\log_2e+(\nu+1)\log_2\kappa+o(1),\\
 S_N&=\log_2N+\log_2(\kappa-1)+\log_2e+g(\nu)+o(1).
\end{align}
Subtracting yields Eq.~\eqref{eq:amp-plob}.

For additive noise, $\tau=1$, $v=\xi$, and
\begin{equation}
 u=\sqrt{\xi N}-\frac{1+\xi}{2}+O(N^{-1/2}),\qquad
 w=u+\xi,\qquad t=N-u.
 \label{eq:add-root-limit}
\end{equation}
Both symplectic photon numbers now diverge as $\sqrt N$. In particular,
$u(u+1+\xi)=\xi N$, and
\begin{equation}
 S_N=\log_2N+\log_2\xi+2\log_2e+o(1).
\end{equation}
To evaluate $C_N$, expand $\varphi(N-u)$ and
$\varphi(N+u+1+\xi)$ about $N$. The net linear displacement after
subtracting $\varphi(N+\xi)$ is $1$. Since
$\varphi''(N)=1/(N\ln2)$ and $u^2/N\to\xi$, the quadratic terms
contribute $\xi\log_2e$; the remaining terms vanish. Thus
\begin{equation}
 C_N=\log_2N+(1+\xi)\log_2e+o(1).
\end{equation}
The difference is Eq.~\eqref{eq:add-plob}. Together with monotonicity,
these three limits prove $\cF(N)\le\Phi$ and complete the limiting
claims in Theorem~\ref{thm:gaussian}. The vanishing-excess-noise
boundaries are consistent with the quantum-limited capacity results of
Ref.~\cite{LossAmplifier}; no boundary-channel
capacity proof is needed here.

\subsection{Hashing and the residual gap}\label{app:hashing}
The output of a two-mode squeezed vacuum has covariance matrix
\begin{equation}
 V_{RB}=\begin{pmatrix}
 (2N+1)I_2&2\sqrt{\tau N(N+1)}Z\\
 2\sqrt{\tau N(N+1)}Z&(2y+1)I_2
 \end{pmatrix},\qquad Z=\operatorname{diag}(1,-1).
 \label{eq:gaussian-covariance}
\end{equation}
Its symplectic eigenvalues are $2u+1$ and $2w+1$, in either order.
The finite-dimensional hashing theorem~\cite{DevetakWinter} therefore
gives Eq.~\eqref{eq:hashing} after cutoff removal. We use the optical
truncations of Ref.~\cite[Appendices B 4 and E 4]{LossAmplifier}
and verify the required entropy convergence for the present noisy
outputs as follows. Truncate
the squeezed input and normalize it, which decreases its mean, and
apply a local vacuum-replacement cutoff to Bob's output. The resulting
finite-dimensional states converge to $\omega_N$, with convergent
reference, output, and joint mean photon numbers. Their entropies converge:
lower semicontinuity gives one direction, while lower semicontinuity
of relative entropy to a fixed full-rank product thermal state gives
the other because its logarithm is affine in the number operators.
Fixing these cutoffs before taking the hashing blocklength limit
achieves any strictly smaller positive rate in Eq.~\eqref{eq:hashing}
within the same input budget and finite-round model.

The gap to the untruncated hashing expression
$I_{\rm hash}=\max\{g(N),g(y)\}-g(u)-g(w)$ has the useful exact form
\begin{equation}
 \cF_{\tau,v}(N)-I_{\rm hash}(N)
   =\int_t^{\min(N,y)}\log_2\frac{L-z}{z}\,\dd z>0
 \label{eq:hashing-gap}
\end{equation}
for $N>0$ and $\max\{0,\tau-1\}<v<\tau$. Indeed,
$t<\min(N,y)$ in this region, and the derivative of
$\varphi(z)+\varphi(L-z)$ is $\log_2[z/(L-z)]$. At the upper integration
endpoint the cross-entropy expression is $\max\{g(N),g(y)\}$.
This proves the identity and positivity. The achievable benchmark
itself is $[I_{\rm hash}]_+$, so Eq.~\eqref{eq:hashing-gap} should not be
read as a gap formula for the clipped rate when $I_{\rm hash}<0$.

